{
  "dom_id": "step-q344749",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/e1aafeb4abd5ee1ee545474ed353bccbfb0986cefccb33779fa5744d735d1bdd.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is inflated at the rate of $48{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If at a particular moment the balloon's radius is changing at the rate $\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{\u00a0cm/s},$ then what is the radius of the balloon at this moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $r$ when $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{\u00a0cm/s}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi (r)^{2} \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius at that moment is $4\\text{\u00a0}\\text{\u00a0cm}.$",
